const DD_MANAGER_PROFILES = {
  "Trey Hugen": {
    headline: `One Ring, One Historic Night, One Long Hangover`,
    narrative: `Let's start with the good stuff: Trey Hugen posted 151.48 points in the 2023 semifinal — the highest single-game score in Duck Dynasty playoff history — and followed it up with a 146.36 championship win over Aaron Fay. Dominant. Convincing. Franchise-defining. And then... nothing. No finals appearances since. That's the Trey Hugen mystery in a nutshell. The talent is undeniable — he carries the highest scoring average in the entire league — but he's leaving 17.47 points per week on his bench, which is the worst lineup efficiency number in DD history. Seventeen points. That's a flex starter just sitting there every Sunday while Trey's clicking submit and moving on. When he's setting an optimized lineup, he's probably the most dangerous roster in the pond. That \`probably\` is doing a lot of work. The Kansas City Chiefs pre-Mahomes comparison is earned: electric ceiling, one ring, hasn't figured out how to make it a dynasty yet. In 2026, the question isn't whether the talent is there. It's whether he'll finally stop leaving a starting tight end on the bench in week nine.`,
  },

  "Aaron Fay": {
    headline: `The Best Regular Season in DD History. Still No Ring.`,
    narrative: `Thirteen and one. Let that sit for a second. Aaron Fay went 13-1 in the 2023 Duck Dynasty regular season — the best record this league has ever seen — and then lost the championship to Trey Hugen by 43 points. That's the Aaron Fay experience in one sentence. He also holds the longest winning streak in DD history at ten games, which somehow makes the championship loss sting even more. The guy is genuinely excellent at fantasy football for fourteen weeks a year. Playoffs, though? Different story. 2024 was another strong regular season that ended in a Round 1 exit — he lost to Vance Sipma in the first round and finished 5th after winning the consolation game. 2025 he missed the playoffs entirely, which has to be alarming after back-to-back dominant regular seasons. His lineup efficiency sits at 93.9% — solid, not exceptional — and he's leaving a flex-worthy player on his bench most weeks. The ceiling is obviously there. A 13-1 season doesn't happen by accident. But in dynasty, rings are the only currency that matters, and Aaron's wallet is empty. The Atlanta Falcons were up 28-3 in Super Bowl LI and blew it. Aaron went 13-1 and lost the championship. Same energy, different sport. 2026 is the year the narrative either shifts or calcifies permanently into \`great regular season manager, couldn't close.\``,
  },

  "Greg Mulder": {
    headline: `98.7% Efficient, One Championship, and One Baffling Disappearing Act`,
    narrative: `Greg Mulder is the most disciplined lineup manager in Duck Dynasty history — 98.7% efficiency, leaving less than a point per week on his bench — and he has a championship ring to prove that attention to detail pays off. The 2024 title run was methodical: top seed, cleared the bracket, won the championship over Vance Sipma by 3.54 points in the closest title game this league has ever played. Classic Mulder. Tight, efficient, no wasted reps. And then 2025 happened. He went from top seed to not even making the playoffs, lost a first-round toilet bowl game to Aaron Fay, and essentially vanished from the conversation. The single biggest cliff drop in DD history in terms of regular season performance. Is it a roster aging issue? Injury luck? Whatever it is, it's the only question mark on an otherwise bulletproof dynasty profile. The Philadelphia Eagles comparison earns its keep: disciplined build, won it methodically in 2024, and then fell off a cliff the next year — just like Philly teams tend to do between runs. The question is whether 2025 was a reset or a warning sign. If the 2025 collapse was a blip, Mulder is a title contender in 2026. If it's a trend, the rebuild starts now.`,
  },

  "Jake Beukelman": {
    headline: `From the Toilet Bowl to the Trophy Case in One Season`,
    narrative: `Okay, let's talk about the arc. In 2024, Jake Beukelman was deep in the toilet bowl bracket, posting big scores like 134.86 and 134.68 in rounds that literally didn't matter. Talented roster, wrong bracket. The 2025 version of Jake was a completely different story — top seed, dominant semifinal performance of 158.58 against Steve Vander Molen (his personal best), and a championship win over Jared Stuit 136.52 to 106.94. From the toilet bowl to the podium in twelve months. That kind of turnaround doesn't happen without serious roster work in the offseason, and Jake clearly put in the time. He's active on the waiver wire, builds depth, and doesn't panic. The lineup efficiency is solid without being elite, and the scheduling gods have been reasonably kind to him. The Los Angeles Rams comparison is apt: aggressive roster moves, a dramatic championship run that nobody fully saw coming, and now the real test of whether the assets are there to sustain it. The real test for 2026 is whether he can defend — or whether the champion's target on his back, combined with the rookie draft picks everyone's chasing, makes a repeat feel unlikely. First-time champions defending the title is the hardest thing to do in dynasty.`,
  },

  "Jared Stuit": {
    headline: `The Greatest Turnaround in Duck Dynasty History. By a Mile.`,
    narrative: `Nobody in this league has a story like Jared Stuit, and it's not particularly close. The man holds the longest losing streak in DD history at fifteen consecutive losses spanning 2023 and 2024. He went 1-13 in the 2024 regular season — the worst record ever in this league — and won the 2023 toilet bowl outright. Then 2024 arrived — 1-13, lost the toilet bowl final to Tyler Goslinga, finished 11th. Absolute rock bottom. And then 2025 happened. Eleven and three. Semifinals win over Trey Hugen. Runner-up finish after losing the championship to Jake Beukelman 106.94 to 136.52. The most dramatic single-season turnaround in Duck Dynasty history, and it's not even debatable. What changed? Everything. The roster got younger, the waiver wire activity picked up, and suddenly the team that was historically bad was one of the most dangerous in the bracket. Here's the honest question heading into 2026: was that a genuine rebuild completing, or was it a one-year fluke propped up by favorable scheduling? The performance was real. The question is sustainability. If Jared is a genuine contender now, this league just got a lot more interesting. If 2025 was an outlier, he's got some explaining to do. The Jacksonville Jaguars went from the worst team in the league to the AFC Championship game in one year — and then faded back into mediocrity. The parallel is uncomfortable and very much on the table.`,
  },

  "Christian Manes": {
    headline: `Inherited a Mess, Still Looking for the Exit`,
    narrative: `Context matters here. Christian Manes took over a roster that Christian Manes ran for one season in 2023, going 4-10 before leaving the league. That's not a foundation — that's a reconstruction project. Christian has been in the league for two seasons and is still clearly in the process of figuring out what this team is. The 2024 season was genuinely encouraging: he made the playoffs, pulled off an upset win over Ross Van Groningen in the first round, and then ran into the Greg Mulder buzzsaw in the semifinal and got demolished 71.46 to 144.30. Brutal. 2025 was a step back — missed the playoffs and made it all the way to the toilet bowl final, where Tyler Goslinga beat him 68.56 to 87.92. Christian scored 87.92 and still couldn't avoid finishing 11th. Cold comfort. Lineup efficiency sits at roughly 88%, meaning he's leaving meaningful points on the bench most weeks. The Arizona Cardinals comp is apt: still searching for an identity, flashes of competitiveness, but not yet a consistent threat. The good news is he's young in this league and the roster can be rebuilt through the draft. 2026 needs to show growth.`,
  },

  "Ross Van Groningen": {
    headline: `Back-to-Back Playoffs, Zero Titles, and One Toilet Bowl Season.`,
    narrative: `Three seasons. One toilet bowl, two playoff appearances. Zero titles. Zero finals appearances. The 2023 debut was not the launch pad anyone hoped for — Ross finished in the bottom six and spent the postseason in the toilet bowl bracket. 2024 was better: he made the playoffs, lost to Christian Manes in an R1 upset that genuinely stung, and immediately fell into the toilet bowl bracket. 2025 was another playoff appearance that ended fast — Trey Hugen handled him 146.16 to 100.66 in the first round. His career luck score is the highest among active DD managers, meaning he's winning more games than his raw scoring output should produce. The schedule has been kind. The results still haven't come. His lineup efficiency at around 94% is solid — he's not making obvious mistakes — and the roster isn't an embarrassment. It's just not competing at the level of Trey, Greg, Aaron, or Jake. The Buffalo Bills made four consecutive Super Bowls and lost them all. Ross has made two consecutive playoffs and hasn't sniffed a final. Different sport, same energy. Still running the league everyone else is winning. 2026 is year four of the project.`,
  },

  "James Lazette": {
    headline: `League-Average Scoring, Below-Average Results, Maximum Frustration`,
    narrative: `James Lazette scores consistently. That's the nice way to say it. The less nice way: three seasons in Duck Dynasty and he's yet to make a meaningful run in the winners bracket. In 2023 he got bounced by Greg Mulder in the first round 102.08 to 134.24. In 2024 he missed the playoffs entirely and posted 133.66 in the losers bracket — starter-quality scoring completely wasted in a toilet bowl round. That's the kind of stat line that makes you stare at the ceiling at night. 2025 he made it back to the playoffs and lost R1 to Steve Vander Molen 131.84 to 149.98, then bounced around the losers bracket before being eliminated. The pattern is consistent: above-average scoring that produces below-average results. His lineup efficiency is around 91%, leaving roughly 11 points per week on the bench, which is an area that could be tightened. The Minnesota Vikings comparison fits cleanly: four Super Bowl appearances, zero rings, perpetually in the conversation and perpetually short. James keeps showing up, scores consistently, and hasn't found the thing that separates good from great in dynasty. James needs a genuine deep run in 2026 or the narrative starts to harden permanently.`,
  },

  "Steve Vander Molen": {
    headline: `13-1. Still No Ring. Most Unlucky Manager in DD History.`,
    narrative: `Here's a sentence that should not exist: Steve Vander Molen went 13-1 in the 2025 Duck Dynasty regular season — the best single-season record in league history — and didn't win the championship. He lost the semifinal to Jake Beukelman 113.30 to 158.58 and the third-place game to Trey Hugen 94.58 to 118.32. Historic regular season, fourth-place finish. The luck delta data confirms what the eye test suggests: Steve loses more games than his scoring output should allow, making him the most consistently unlucky manager in the pond. In 2023 and 2024 he wasn't even close to the playoffs. Then he went from irrelevant to historically dominant in one offseason. That jump is real and the roster construction behind it deserves credit. The Detroit Lions 2023 comparison is unavoidable and uncomfortably accurate: best record in the league, nothing to show for it. The lineup efficiency needs work at around 90%, and 12 points per week sitting on the bench in a league this competitive is a problem. But the foundation is clearly there. Steve Vander Molen is a legitimate championship contender in 2026. He just needs the playoffs to cooperate for once.`,
  },

  "Vance Sipma": {
    headline: `Lost the Championship by 3.54 Points. Lives There Rent-Free.`,
    narrative: `Three point five four. That's the margin of the 2024 Duck Dynasty championship game. Vance Sipma built what might have been the most dominant team in the bracket that year — he beat Trey Hugen in the semifinal 151.28 to 108.10, which is a demolition — and then lost the title to Greg Mulder by less than a field goal. The Cincinnati Bengals comparison is the right one. The talent has been there. The results keep finding a way to disappoint. What makes 2025 even harder to process is the collapse: from runner-up to missing the playoffs entirely, with a 63.52-point performance in a losers bracket game that was one of the worst scores of the season. His lineup efficiency sits at 89.6% with 12.52 points per week on the bench — that's the gap. The Bengals got to the Super Bowl and lost. Vance got to the championship and lost by 3.54 points. In a league where the title came down to 3.54 points, leaving 12 on the bench every week is the difference between a ring and a footnote. If Vance tightens the lineup management and gets back to a dominant regular season, he's clearly a finals-caliber team. The 2024 run proved the ceiling. The 2025 collapse is the warning. 2026 needs an answer.`,
  },

  "Will Hoekstra": {
    headline: `Three Seasons, Zero Winners Bracket Playoff Appearances`,
    narrative: `Will Hoekstra has been in Duck Dynasty for three seasons and has not once appeared in the winners bracket playoff rounds. Every postseason he's been in the losers bracket — the toilet bowl side — posting respectable scores without advancing past the first or second round. 133.78 in 2023. 92.02 in 2024. 114.50 in 2025. Those aren't embarrassing numbers. They're just not enough to advance when you're starting from the bottom of the league. His lineup efficiency is around 91%, which is average, and the waiver wire activity has been high — he's clearly trying to upgrade — but the roster hasn't reached the depth needed to compete for a playoff spot in a twelve-team Superflex league. This isn't a management problem; it's a roster construction problem. The talent level on this team isn't championship caliber yet. The path forward in dynasty is clear: prioritize the rookie draft, target younger players with upside, and stop chasing short-term waiver adds that don't move the needle. Will has the energy and attention — the 2026 draft class could be the turning point if he approaches it with a plan. The Tennessee Titans always seemed like they were about to turn the corner and never quite did. Year four is the moment to prove this is different.`,
  },

  "Tyler Goslinga": {
    headline: `Back-to-Back Toilet Bowl Champion. Not a Brag.`,
    narrative: `Let's just say it plainly: Tyler Goslinga has won the Duck Dynasty Toilet Bowl in back-to-back seasons (2024 and 2025), making him the only manager in league history to hold that distinction twice — and consecutively. In the toilet bowl, lower score advances, which means Tyler was consistently the worst-scoring team in the bracket in both years. The 2023 season wasn't much better. Three years in this league and the results have been consistently poor. What makes it more puzzling is the transaction volume — Tyler is constantly on the waiver wire, constantly shuffling pieces, constantly adding and dropping — but the roster hasn't improved in any visible way. High churn without direction is just spinning wheels. His lineup efficiency sits around 91%, so it's not a lineup management issue driving the bad records; it's the underlying roster quality. The Cleveland Browns comp was earned through consistent bottom-of-standings finishes rather than any philosophical similarity, and that's a perfectly valid reason. The path forward requires a complete reset in approach: stop adding veterans, target the 2026 and 2027 rookie drafts deliberately, and build a young core. The talent to be competitive exists in this league. Tyler just needs to find a different way to get there.`,
  },
};

